    "result_text": "Both findings addressed, tests green. Summary of what I changed:\n\n**Primary finding (SHA-256\u2192hex duplication) \u2014 agreed and fixed.** Extracted a single `sha256Hex(String input, int bytes, boolean upperCase)` helper that owns the `MessageDigest`/try-catch/`IllegalStateException` and the hex-rendering loop. Both call sites now read:\n- `computeHouseholdId` \u2192 `sha256Hex(key, 6, false)`\n- `customerHash` \u2192 `sha256Hex(telephone + lastName, 4, true)`\n\nThe digest handling and the \"SHA-256 not available\" error now live in one place.\n\n**Secondary finding (triplicated key extraction) \u2014 agreed and fixed.** Introduced a `householdKey(owner)` helper returning `normalizedLastName + '|' + postcode` (or `null`), and routed all three sites through it:\n- `computeHouseholdId` hashes the shared key.\n- `findHouseholdMembers` filters by `key.equals(householdKey(existing))`.\n- `findPossibleDuplicate` reuses the same key plus its different-telephone check.\n\nThis guarantees the \"same keying\" invariant between membership matching and id computation can't drift, and removes the repeated `lastName == null || postcode == null || postcode.isBlank()` guard.\n\nBehaviour is unchanged (the hash of `lastName + \"|\" + postcode` and `telephone + lastName` are byte-for-byte identical to before), and the acceptance test still passes: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
