    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe change is largely well-structured \u2014 the new `HouseholdDuplicateException` mirrors the existing `DuplicateIdentityException`/limit exceptions, the advice handler is a faithful copy of the sibling 409 handlers, and `computeHouseholdId`/`findHouseholdMembers` are named and documented consistently with the surrounding code. The logic sits in the right layer (service enforces the invariant, REST maps it). But there is one real, newly-introduced duplication worth addressing:\n\n**SHA-256 \u2192 hex boilerplate is now duplicated in the same class.** `computeHouseholdId` (lines 415\u2013426) and the pre-existing `customerHash` (lines 457\u2013468) are near-identical:\n- `MessageDigest.getInstance(\"SHA-256\").digest(...getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8))`\n- the same `catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e)` \u2192 `throw new IllegalStateException(\"SHA-256 is required but not available\", e)`\n- the same `StringBuilder` + `String.format(\"%02x\"/\"%02X\")` loop\n\nThey differ only in input string, number of bytes (6 vs 4), and hex case. This change turns a one-off into a repeated pattern. Extracting a single private helper, e.g.:\n\n```java\nprivate String sha256Hex(String input, int bytes, boolean upperCase) { ... }\n```\n\nwould let both call sites read `sha256Hex(lastName + \"|\" + postcode, 6, false)` and `sha256Hex(telephone + lastName, 4, true)`, removing the duplicated digest/try-catch/loop and keeping the \"SHA-256 not available\" handling in one place. A maintainer touching the error handling or algorithm would otherwise have to find and update both copies.\n\nSecondary (minor, optional): the `(normalizedLastName, postcode)` extraction plus the `lastName == null || postcode == null || postcode.isBlank()` guard now appears three times (`findHouseholdMembers`, `computeHouseholdId`, `findPossibleDuplicate`). Since `findHouseholdMembers` is documented as using exactly the same keying as `computeHouseholdId`, consider having `findHouseholdMembers` reuse the computed id (or a shared key-extraction helper) rather than re-deriving the key independently \u2014 it keeps the \"same keying\" invariant from drifting between the two methods. Not blocking on its own.",
